 ## Overview
-Adaptive Stream Line Seeding is a GPU-based algorithm that dynamically refines the density of stream lines in 3D vector fields by subdividing regions where neighboring lines diverge beyond a threshold [S1, unofficial]. Unlike static seeding methods that distribute seeds uniformly or based on pre-computed scalar fields, this method iteratively inserts new seeds between existing diverging line pairs to capture turbulent or vortex structures while maintaining sparsity in laminar flow areas [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Stream Lines](4796-stream-lines.md) by adding a control phase that manages seed placement based on geometric divergence.
+Adaptive Stream Line Seeding is a method for visualizing vector fields by dynamically increasing the density of streamlines in regions where the flow diverges, such as turbulent areas or vortices, while maintaining sparse sampling in laminar regions [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [Directional Textured Tracelines](6965-directional-textured-tracelines.md) and [Stream Surfaces](7696-stream-surfaces.md) by explicitly using geometric divergence between neighboring line segments to trigger the insertion of new seed points, thereby creating a denser surface representation of the flow structure [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The algorithm operates in an iterative loop managed by the CPU, alternating between a control kernel (which identifies diverging line pairs and generates new seeds) and an evaluation kernel (which integrates the stream lines) [S1, unofficial]. The control phase uses atomic operations to manage dynamic arrays within fixed-size GPU buffers, allowing the number of lines to grow until a capacity limit is reached [S1, unofficial].
+The algorithm operates iteratively on the GPU, typically alternating between a control kernel that identifies diverging line pairs and an evaluation kernel that integrates the new streamlines [S1, unofficial]. The core mechanism involves analyzing pairs of existing streamlines (or line segments) and inserting new seed points between them if the distance between corresponding points exceeds a defined threshold [S2, unofficial].
 
